          "ruleId": "CWE-937",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "Dependency enumeration for this scan (components-with-known-vulnerabilities class): declared third-party dependencies are normalize-url ^7.0.3, parse-path ^7.0.0, pkgroll ^1.4.0, and tester ^1.3.1. No CVE/advisory database lookup tool was available in this session's allowed toolset (only run_cypher/semantic_search), so I could not confirm any of these as outdated/EOL/known-vulnerable from queried data alone -- surfacing the full declared set here so an independent verifier with vulnerability-DB access can check each name@version against current advisories (normalize-url and parse-path are runtime deps reachable from the parseUrl entry point's data flow; pkgroll and tester appear to be build/test tooling only).\n\nVerifier (INCONCLUSIVE): I independently re-ran the analyst's Cypher query and it returned the same 4 dependency rows. I then cross-checked against the real source: /tmp/workspaces/33995ded263630b3/tree/package.json confirms the exact same declarations \u2014 dependencies: normalize-url ^7.0.3, parse-path ^7.0.0 (lines 43-46); devDependencies: pkgroll ^1.4.0, tester ^1.3.1, normalize-url ^7.0.3 (lines 38-42). So the factual substrate of the lead (the dependency list itself) is real and accurately reported, not fabricated or mis-attributed. However, the lead as framed makes no actual vulnerability claim \u2014 the analyst explicitly states they could not confirm any of these as outdated/EOL/known-vulnerable. I attempted to close that gap myself: mcp__orion__exploit_search (the only vulnerability-reference tool available to me) returned \"exploit corpus unavailable: not indexed\" for all four package names, so I have no in-session tool evidence of a CVE/advisory matching these exact declared version ranges. There is also no lockfile (package-lock.json/pnpm-lock.yaml) or node_modules in the tree, so I cannot even pin the exact resolved versions beyond the semver ranges in package.json \u2014 ^7.0.3/^7.0.0 could resolve to any current 7.x patch. Without a working CVE database/advisory lookup and without a pinned exact version, I cannot establish that any of these components is actually vulnerable, nor can I rule it out. This matches a genuine evidence gap rather than a disprovable claim, so per the rules I cannot CONFIRM (no concrete vulnerability evidence) and should not REJECT either (the dependency data itself is accurate and the packages are real, current third-party components whose vuln status is simply unverifiable with the tools available this session)."
          },
